  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nOvernight Storms & Heavy Rain Potential:\n\nThe low level boundary, evident in a southwest to northwest \nwind shift on low level VWPs between the Mississippi and \nMissouri River Valleys, will continue instigating storms along \na parallel (southwest to northeast) orientation overnight. A \nslight eastern shift in the backing wind profiles resulted in \nthe line of heaviest rainfall and strongest storms slowly shifting\neast of the local forecast area into central Wisconsin through \nthe early in the overnight hours. However, a more northerly \ncomponent of the trajectory to parent mid-upper level low over \nsouthern Canada overnight is gradually decreasing southerly \nadvection of the boundary through Tuesday morning, raising \nslight concern for reinitiation of storms capable of heavy rain \nnorthwest of current (15.08Z) RADAR imagery where heavy \nrainfall totals nearing 2\" have been observed. Therefore, \novernight flooding concerns cannot be completely ruled out until\nthe frontal completely exits the area through early Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nDrier & Cool Today, Limited Rainfall Potential:\n\nThe aforementioned upper level low eventually abandons the boundary \nalong the southeastern periphery of the local forecast area through \nTuesday. Exact southern extent will determine lingering heavy rain \npotential in select peripheral counties as storms potentially burble \nalong the boundary until anticyclonic flow further shunts moisture \nsouthward into Tuesday night. Outside of the select counties in \nnortheast Iowa into far southwest Wisconsin, a drier, cooler \nupstream airmass, evident in overnight surface dewpoint observations \nin the mid to low 50s and temperatures from the upper 50s to low 60s \nat 15.06Z spreads east across the forecast area through today. \nDaytime temperatures from the upper 60s along our northwestern \nperipheral counties to low 70s elsewhere expected.\n\nMidweek Heavy Rain & Storm Potential:\n\nLow level anticyclonic flow traverses the forecast area overnight \ninto Wednesday, resulting in return flow returning the warm, moist \nairmass across the Upper Mississippi River Valley into \nWednesday night. Confidence in local precipitation chances \nremains low- moderate (20-60%) as GEFS (15.00Z) presents a drier\nsolution than the GEPS/EPS due differences in strength of upper\nlevel jet streak providing enhanced ageostrophic ascent within \nthe equatorward entrance region, an accompanying lobe of mid \nlevel shear vorticity, and entrained drier air in the mid levels\naccording to associated individual ensemble member sounding \nplumes. Should the stronger forcing solution in the GEPS/EPS \ncome to fruition, another 0.5\" - 1\" of rainfall will be possible\novernight Wednesday into Thursday morning. While overall \namounts aren't overly concerning, potential subsequent flooding \nimpacts into the weekend will be impacted by this supplementary \nmidweek rainfall.\n\nHeavy Rain & Flooding Concerns Through Friday:\n\nSynoptic confluence through late week funnels the anomalous moisture \nin a narrow corridor, similar albeit slightly lower than\ncurrent areal observations, through Friday into Saturday with \nLREF probabilities for 2\" PWATs decreasing/increasing and \nshifting south/north in most recent runs of the GEFS/EPS placing\ntemporarily maxima of 40%/70% near the southwestern half of the \nforecast area. While the longer forecast hour combined with \naforementioned low-moderate confidence rainfall midweek limits \noverall confidence in flooding concerns, LREF sounding plumes \nsuggest anomalous freezing levels and thick warm cloud depths, \nreraising concern for areal and river flooding potential late \nThursday night through Friday into Saturday.",
